From-scratch reboot. The previous material is left untouched on main and is
now reference only. No manuscripts existed, so nothing was discarded at prose
level.

Design decisions taken:

- Escalation engine is dependency, not opposition. He wants an east that never
  needs permission from anyone; his method is solving everything himself; so
  every enemy he removes leaves behind people who no longer have to handle the
  next problem. Escalation is counted in people, not enemy size.

- The exile is load-bearing. He remembers a world that ended and can never say
  so, because the Ash burns people for claiming another life. The garden is
  what a homesick man builds when he cannot go home. Grief is not fear, guilt,
  or strain, so this survives the no-cost rule.

- The Hollow is now BUILT by him in volume 1, one recruit at a time, not
  inherited. No ancient order, no First Watch, nothing to investigate. The
  Keeping survives as folk texture only. The Hollow is explicitly feared, not
  scrappy: its method is absence, it never loses in front of the reader, and
  its reputation escalates across five volumes in enemy dialogue.

- The Council Beat is mandatory once per volume: the enemy's leadership in
  session and competent, Casian in the room unnoticed, then he speaks once.
  Fixes the old flaw of only ever showing the men he dealt with while the real
  leadership stayed offstage as weather. Inverts in vol 3 (Ilse sees him first)
  and cannot be repeated in vol 5.

- Romance is one lane: Seraine. Vesna and Sabeth are removed from romance and
  given structural jobs instead — Vesna is the counter-evidence to dependency,
  Sabeth is the question he cannot charm his way out of.

- Ages are now generated. canon/characters.json is the single source of truth
  and tools/age_ledger.py generates roster.txt and ages.md, with --check for
  staleness and --check-year to audit chapter frontmatter. Absolute calendar
  1217-1222. This is the structural fix for the age drift that broke every
  previous draft.

- Five volumes, each a different kind of story: intimate, institutional, a
  peer, social, foundational.

